        "id": "05",
        "name": "05 / Moss",
        "description": "Sow moss on a white faceted boulder, or on one big rounded letter or digit, and watch it settle in. Every object starts bare, the rock on load and anything you pick: all the moss on it is yours. The button top left shows a live miniature of the current object, moss and all, with a moss meter ring around it: the share of reachable surface covered by mature moss (age over 0.55, so the thin rim of a stroke does not count), area-weighted, not counting anything that faces down or infertile spots. Cover 80 % of the rock and the alphabet unlocks: the ring fades away, and the miniature opens a picker; until then there is no picker, and no numbers anywhere. Each glyph in the picker is its own miniature: a low-detail bare rounded letter in the rock's white material, no moss, rendered one per frame in the background, green text standing in until it lands. The unlock is remembered in localStorage. The picker has the rock and every glyph in the three.js Helvetiker typeface, regular weight: rounding and moss thicken every stroke, and in bold the small counters (A, e, 4) close up. The default view is from the front with the eye almost on the ground. Everything floats: the ground sits a gap below the object's lowest point, 0.25 for letters and 0.4 for the rock so the moss round its belly never touches. The camera fits the object's height into 70 % of the viewport; the rock also fits 95 % of the width, so it works on a portrait phone. Drag on the rock to sow, shift-drag (or right-drag) to scrape it off, drag outside the rock, or hold space and drag anywhere, to orbit the camera (the cursor is a cross where a drag paints and a hand where it orbits); on a phone one finger paints, two fingers orbit and pinch zooms in a little. A small \"two fingers to rotate\" hint sits just under the object and fades out after the first two-finger turn. The default brush is feather, the leftmost swatch. The mix brush is auto: it mixes species by the surface normal (tops get cushion, sides feather, undersides dark star moss, lichen in its own patchy field), by stroke speed (slow strokes press in thick cushion, fast ones leave thin feather and lichen), by a slow time drift, and by a random bias rolled per stroke. The swatch row at the bottom, in order feather, star, cushion, lichen, coral, mix, maps to keys 1 to 6. Hovering a swatch shows the real plant it is modelled on: *Hylocomium splendens* (glittering wood-moss), *Polytrichum commune* (common haircap), *Leucobryum glaucum* (pincushion moss), *Cladonia rangiferina* (grey reindeer lichen) and *Cladonia arbuscula* (green reindeer lichen). The last two are lichens, not mosses. These are the closest real models, not exact matches. The eraser at the end of the swatch row (or X, or shift-drag) scrapes, since there is no shift key on a phone. Cmd-drag (ctrl on Windows) sideways sets the brush size, a ring shows it; it starts medium. The moss itself is scaled to 175 %, a constant `mossScale` that covers frond and straw lengths, fuzz height and frequency, bulge. H hides the rock so you can look at the moss alone. A small settings panel top right, collapsed by default, toggles the back light and the sky ambient, switches AO on and off (the SSAO pass and the darkening of moss in hollows, remembered across visits), picks the tone mapping, and has a fill button that covers the whole object with the current brush at once, for comparing looks.",
